Why each element was added
---------------------------

3 firewalls
  One in front of the load balancer, one in front of each server. They filter
  incoming and outgoing traffic, blocking anything that shouldn't be there
  (unwanted ports, suspicious IPs, etc.).

SSL certificate
  Installed on the load balancer so that traffic between users and the
  infrastructure is encrypted over HTTPS. Without it, data travels in plain text.

3 monitoring clients
  One on the load balancer and one on each server. They collect metrics, logs,
  and events and ship them to a monitoring service (like Sumologic) so you have
  visibility into what's happening across the whole infrastructure.


Q&A
----

What are firewalls for?
  They act as gatekeepers. They inspect network traffic and block anything that
  doesn't match the allowed rules — wrong port, unknown source, malicious pattern.
  They reduce the attack surface by making sure only intended traffic gets through.

Why serve traffic over HTTPS?
  HTTPS encrypts the connection between the user and the server using TLS.
  This protects sensitive data (passwords, tokens, personal info) from being
  intercepted or tampered with in transit. It also builds user trust and is
  required by modern browsers for many features.

What is monitoring used for?
  To know what's happening on your infrastructure in real time. It tracks things
  like server health, response times, error rates, CPU/memory usage, and traffic.
  When something breaks or degrades, monitoring is what alerts you before users
  start complaining.

How does the monitoring tool collect data?
  Each monitoring client runs as an agent on the server. It reads system metrics,
  application logs, and process stats, then ships that data to the central
  monitoring service (Sumologic) at regular intervals or in real time.

How to monitor web server QPS (queries per second)?
  Configure the monitoring client to read Nginx's access logs or its built-in
  stub_status metrics. The agent parses request counts over time and reports QPS
  to the monitoring service, where you can set up dashboards and alerts.


Issues with this infrastructure
---------------------------------

Terminating SSL at the load balancer is a problem
  Once the load balancer decrypts the traffic, it forwards requests to the backend
  servers over plain HTTP. That means traffic inside the infrastructure is
  unencrypted. If the internal network is compromised, that data is exposed.
  Ideally, encryption should be maintained end-to-end, all the way to the servers.

Only one MySQL server can accept writes
  The Primary is the only node that handles writes. If it goes down, no write
  operations can happen until a failover is completed — the site breaks for anything
  that needs to write data. There's no redundancy for the write path.

All servers have the same components (web + app + database on each)
  This creates resource competition — the database, app server, and web server all
  fight for the same CPU and RAM. It also makes scaling harder: if you need more
  database capacity, you have to scale the whole server instead of just that layer.
  It also means a problem in one component (like a memory leak in the app) can
  affect the database running on the same machine.


